# Written here rather than committed so the bytes are the ones the case means.
#
# Two of them are named with a leading dot, which is how this harness is told
# not to list a file. A recording renders contents through `str::lines` and
# reads them back with a newline restored, so neither a file that ends without
# one nor a file that is nothing but one can be recorded as itself. They are
# inputs rather than answers, and the answers they produce are in `out`.
printf 'a\n' > one-newline
printf 'a\n\n' > two-newlines
printf 'a\n\n\n' > three-newlines
printf 'a' > .no-newline
printf '\n' > .only-newline
printf '' > empty
